# DDGO 3D UI Toolkit Capability Test

## Purpose

Create several completely independent experimental applications to determine which UI toolkits can support the core graphical requirement for DDGO:

**An ordinary desktop UI containing an interactive 3D toolpath view.**

These applications are experiments only. They are not part of DDGO's production application architecture and should not depend on DDGO application code.

The same basic program should be implemented with several toolkits and, where useful, several languages.

## Repository Location

Keep all experiments under one isolated directory:

```text
experiments/ui3d/
```

Each implementation gets its own self-contained directory:

```text
experiments/ui3d/
├── qt/
├── <toolkit-2>/
├── <toolkit-3>/
└── ...
```

Each implementation should:

- be independently buildable
- contain its own dependency/build configuration
- not import code from another UI experiment
- not require changes to DDGO's production code
- not become a dependency of the main DDGO application
- not be included in normal DDGO builds or tests unless explicitly requested

Duplicating small amounts of test code between experiments is preferable to creating a shared abstraction layer.

The objective is to evaluate the toolkits, not design a portable UI abstraction.

## Application

Each experiment consists of **one desktop window**.

The window contains:

1. A 3D viewport occupying most of the window.
2. A small ordinary UI area containing a few controls.

An acceptable layout is:

```text
┌────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                                            │
│                                            │
│              3D VIEWPORT                   │
│                                            │
│                                            │
├────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ [ Reset View ] [ Animate ]   Status/FPS    │
└────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

The exact layout and styling should follow what is natural for the toolkit.

Pixel-identical implementations are not required.

## 3D Scene

The viewport displays a synthetic CNC-style toolpath.

No G-code parser or DDGO program format is involved.

Generate the path programmatically at startup.

The path should contain enough geometry to unmistakably demonstrate three-dimensional motion, including:

- straight XY cutting moves
- Z changes
- several different cutting depths
- at least one curved or circular feature
- rapid/repositioning moves
- a final retract

The test path should be deterministic and substantially identical between implementations.

### Scene Reference Features

Also display:

- X, Y, and Z axes
- an XY grid
- the toolpath
- a marker showing the current simulated tool position

Rapid moves and cutting moves should be visually distinguishable.

No stock model, cutter model, lighting effects, textures, or material-removal simulation are required.

## View Interaction

The user must be able to:

- orbit the camera
- zoom
- pan

The 3D view must resize correctly when the application window is resized.

The specific mouse controls may follow the toolkit or 3D library's conventions.

## UI Controls

Provide at least two normal UI controls outside the 3D renderer.

### Reset View

Returns the camera to the initial useful view of the complete toolpath.

### Animate / Stop

Starts and stops a simple traversal of the generated toolpath.

While animation is running:

- the tool-position marker moves along the path
- the 3D view remains interactive
- the rest of the UI remains responsive

The static path should not be regenerated every animation frame.

A small text label may display current segment number, FPS, or another useful diagnostic value.

## Minimum Rendering Requirement

The initial capability test does **not** need to render millions of segments.

Use enough geometry to demonstrate that a real collection of toolpath geometry is being rendered rather than a trivial three-line demonstration.

Approximately 1,000–10,000 generated line segments is sufficient for the first implementation.

More demanding datasets belong in the comparison stage after the toolkit has demonstrated basic capability.

## Success Criteria

A toolkit passes the initial test if:

- the application builds and launches reproducibly
- the 3D viewport is embedded in the same window as normal UI controls
- the generated path renders correctly in three dimensions
- depth relationships are displayed correctly
- orbit, pan, and zoom work
- Reset View works
- the animated position marker works
- normal UI remains responsive during rendering and animation
- resizing the window correctly resizes the viewport
- the implementation does not require an obviously unreasonable workaround to combine the UI and 3D renderer

## Information to Record

Each implementation should contain a short README recording:

- language
- UI toolkit
- 3D/rendering library or API
- graphics backend where known
- build instructions
- runtime dependencies
- whether CGO or another foreign-language interface is required
- platforms the implementation should theoretically support
- problems or limitations encountered
- any toolkit-specific workaround required to host the 3D viewport

Do not attempt detailed performance comparisons during this first stage.

## Explicitly Out of Scope

Do not implement:

- DDGO integration
- machine communication
- G-code parsing
- DD/DDD parsing
- file loading
- multiple windows
- document architecture
- path selection/picking
- docking
- preferences
- stock simulation
- collision detection
- production styling
- production error handling
- installers
- large-scale performance optimization

These experiments exist only to establish whether each candidate provides a viable foundation for a DDGO UI with interactive 3D graphics.

## Stage 2

Toolkits that pass this test can then be subjected to a common comparison program with:

- large path datasets
- rendering performance measurements
- path selection
- more substantial normal UI
- packaging tests
- cross-platform testing
- implementation complexity comparison

Do not add Stage 2 requirements to an implementation until its Stage 1 capability test works.
